ess arrow memory that that algorithm  is automatically reusable so you know  it's very difficult right now if I write  an algorithm that processes a panda's  data frame I can't take that code and  process an our data frame because the  memory is different  using the same technology we've been  developing tools to enable data to be  shared between processes without copying  so this is about making it really cheap  and easy to access very large data sets  and to blur the boundary between data  and memory and data on disk so if you  have a really huge data set that is on  your laptop on a solid-state drive maybe  it's a terabyte of data maybe it's 100  gigabytes of data maybe there's only a  small chunk of that data that you want  to use to for your analysis with a lot  of data serialization tools in order to  access that subset of data for your  analysis you have to read the whole data  set so if it might be like the middle of  a CSV file so you have to parse the  whole CSV file before you can get that  middle chunk so arrow makes that that  problem go away where you can just you  know access only the data that you need  and you can get access to that very  cheaply we've been using we've been  using arrow to build file formats so you  may have heard of this thing called  feather that that Hadley and I made so  that was a first example of building a  file format that that uses arrow and I  think that I think that we'll see more  of that that sort of thing you know  develop and in the next couple of years  and so I know in the our world there's a  there's another competing file format  with feather called  called FST and so the way I would  contrast or way I would describe FST is  as an evolved version of feather that  can only be read and are and so from my  point of view like having file formats  that can be read in any programming  languages is a you know kind of that's  that's really what I would what I would  like to see one of the biggest the the  project has grown so much is it's no  longer just about data frames and  columnar data but the columnar data  format is one of the biggest parts of  the project where we spent the most time  designing a suitably general and  efficient data frame representation for  for analytics so I won't go through  these details but these are things you  can read more about we've been building  libraries and many programming languages  so we started out with Java and C++ we  now have a native JavaScript  implementation it's really cool it can  you can generate it a narrow file and in  Java or Python and send it to the  browser and read it without doing any  parsing so it doesn't go through JSON or  any of that and and that's really neat  we just received a donation of a go  implementation which was created by  influx data which which builds in flux  DB I need to update this diagram because  we  have a burgeoning rust native rust  implementation which is really cool I  know there's also a Julia implementation  somewhere in the ether that I hope can  get merged into the into the Apache  project and we've been building bindings  so I've been working on and with you  know many other people on Python  bindings there's also Ruby bindings  which are kind of it's like kind of a  conception where it's like bindings on  bindings so Ruby is going through C  which is talking to C++ so you know it's  you know you have bindings - binding -  bindings starting to see arrow used in  some pretty cool applications so if you  follow work happening in the Berkeley  Ryze lab which is oriented around real  time secure machine learning they've  developed a machine learning framework  called ray which uses they developed a  shared memory object store for managing  data that flows between tasks in a in a  reinforcement learning setting and so  that object store is called plasma and  they donated that to the to the arrow  project and it's become a tightly  integrated component so that we can see  more machine learning sys