nd R will make that whole stack  a great deal more powerful so at a high  level you know the way I think about the  problem having worked in this ecosystem  for a long time is that Python and are  built on this computational foundation  that was established in the mid-1990s  we're looking backward from say 1995  everyone was programming in Fortran and  it was you know you said hey wouldn't it  be great if we had interpreted  programming languages that could  manipulate the same code the same linear  algebra libraries multi-dimensional  arrays that we're using in Fortran but  we could do so in an interactive  interpreted environment so hence we had  numpy developed in Python and equivalent  types of tools in R but there's a lot of  problems with the the computational  systems that perform analytics in memory  so a lot of the algorithms are single  threaded there isn't a great deal of you  know the execution model the way that  algorithms are evaluated is is very much  you know one step at a time there's a  lot of temporary data that's produced so  so there's a bunch of problems there and  what's happening is that you look in the  Python Antartica system and people are  you know if your data is small  everything's fine and so it turns out  that a lot of people have small data and  this is just not an issue but as soon as  you start getting into you know  the tens or hundreds of gigabytes of  data you start to get into into  struggles Ville and so and I've been  there and really you think about where  things are going we've got these  machines with tons of RAM tons of very  fast solid-state drives and loss of  cores we should be able to work with 100  gigabyte data sets on a laptop without  even thinking twice about it and so  that's that's the world that I'd like to  I'd like to get to so to have Oliver as  many of our algorithms be multi-core GPU  accelerated we have all this fancy code  generation stuff that we can employ to  make things even even faster there's a  